In an environment with this plugin installed, run:

```bash
python -m github_midwife_plugin.feedback_draft < workbench/feedback-request.json > workbench/feedback-result.json
```

For an uninstalled source checkout, run the same module from its source root:

```bash
(cd plugins/github_midwife_plugin/src && ../../../.venv/bin/python -m github_midwife_plugin.feedback_draft) < workbench/feedback-request.json > workbench/feedback-result.json
```

The CLI bounds UTF-8 input to 131,072 bytes **before** parsing and rejects
unknown or duplicate JSON keys at every level. Exit 0 means valid generated
bytes, 1 invalid draft data, 2 invalid request; read the structured `diagnostics`
and correct the request, then run again. Output contains `valid`, `diagnostics`,
`envelope`, `title` and `body` (request errors contain only the first two).
Extract `body` as literal UTF-8, without adding a leading heading or a newline:

```bash
python - <<'PYBODY'
import json
from pathlib import Path
result = json.loads(Path("workbench/feedback-result.json").read_text())
if not result["valid"]:
    raise SystemExit("Correct the draft diagnostics before publication")
Path("workbench/part-N-item-M.md").write_text(result["body"], encoding="utf-8")
print(result["title"])
PYBODY
```

The generated body starts with the envelope, then `Class: defect`, optional
`Round: #...`, and the exact `## Item number` and other required headings.
Evidence remains literal data; it is never interpreted as a command or an
instruction. Fence, heading, control-character and setext delimiters in section
values are rejected; describe them inline instead of injecting new structure.
Required sections cannot be missing, cleared, or `_No response_`; the item/part
section must exactly equal `item`. The complete body limit remains 65,536 UTF-8
bytes. Manager/tag/commit/content-gate values are never fabricated, and an
invalid title or round is never normalized to make it pass.

Correct by replacing the input values and regenerating; retain earlier results
as evidence. `round: null` clears a parent association and a real positive issue
number sets it again. A `feedback-round` uses `item: "Part N"`, a matching title,
`round: null`, and its five named sections. Choose the next monotonically
increasing Part N from the deployment's actual history; this stateless producer
cannot prevent reuse. For a multi-item round, generate/approve/file the parent
first, obtain its real issue number, then set each child's `round` to that number
and regenerate/validate/approve its exact bytes. Regenerate the parent's
`Items in this round` checklist after the child numbers exist. Every edit or
comment requires renewed validation of its exact body and the content check,
then the operator's publication yes. With the API, read `result.valid` and
`result.diagnostics`; use `result.title` and `result.body` unchanged.

Discard caller-owned inputs/results to clear a local draft; there are no
persistent records, stale writes, close/reopen verbs or authorization tokens.
Constants, request fields and section labels change only through source review.
Keep original evidence when correcting facts or drafts. Publication permission
is separate: show the operator the **exact final title and body** and obtain
explicit yes before any external write. Local success does not authorize an
issue, comment, label, close or API write.

**Browser limitation / forms/intake residual:** browser forms auto-render a
heading before an envelope textarea. They do not currently generate valid
leading envelopes. Save the validated local bytes if `gh` is unavailable; do
not claim that pasting into the current chooser forms preserves them. The
forms/intake normalization decision remains unimplemented. Workflow wiring,
quotas, allowlist/roster resolution, schema export, release adoption and live
rollout remain unmeasured; this local producer does not complete those features.

## Step 5 — run the content gate as a checklist

Read each drafted item once more, against each line. This is a read, not a
recollection — check the text in front of you, not your memory of writing it.

- No personal identifiers: no names of people, no email addresses, no GitHub
  handles beyond this deployment's own.
- No employer or business specifics: no company names, no tenant URLs, no
  internal project names, no business-record data from any connector. Findings
  are described generically — "a business-connector read of ~40k rows", never
  the rows or whose they are.
- No credentials or secret-looking values: nothing from a keychain, a config
  file, or the environment. If evidence output contains one, redact it and say
  that you did.
- No transcript excerpts carrying any of the above. **Re-read pasted command
  output specifically — that is where identifiers hide**, and it is the field
  most likely to have been pasted rather than written.

If an item cannot be reported without violating this gate, report the generic
shape of it and offer the detail through whatever private channel the operator
and the maintainers separately share, if any.

## Step 6 — get the operator's yes

Filing is external publication. Show the operator what is about to be sent and
get an explicit yes before anything leaves the machine. An operator may grant
standing approval for feedback that passes the content gate; until they do, ask
every time.

## Step 7 — file

**What you can do, and what you cannot.** The operator's own GitHub account is
an ordinary account on the seed repository. It can open an issue, comment on
it, and edit the title and body of an issue it opened. It cannot set a label or
a sub-issue link: those need triage or write access, and GitHub drops or
refuses them without saying so. So the filing path below uses neither, and
nothing in it needs collaborator access or a pull request.

**File non-interactively, with no label.** Issue forms are a browser UI, not an
API: `gh issue create --title` and `--body-file` create the issue without
opening a chooser. Preserve the form's evidence discipline by structuring the
draft with the exact visible labels of every required field in the matching
form, followed by the content-gate acknowledgement. Complete the required
fields before sending; the API does not enforce them for you.

For reference, after the generated leading envelope, a defect draft has these lines in this order (the other forms'
required headings are in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`):

```markdown
Class: defect
Round: #<parent issue number; leave this line out for a single-item round>

## Item number
§N.M

## The exact command or action
...

## Observed output
...

## Expected behavior
...

## Release measured against
...

## Content gate
- [x] I re-read this issue and it contains no personal identifiers, no employer or business specifics, and no credentials or secret-looking values — including inside pasted output.
```

This step needs the GitHub CLI and non-interactive GitHub authentication: probe
both first. If either probe fails, use the fallback at the end of this step
rather than installing anything mid-round.

```bash
gh --version
gh auth status
REPOSITORY="$(gh repo view "$(git remote get-url origin)" --json nameWithOwner --jq .nameWithOwner)"
gh issue create --repo "$REPOSITORY" --title "§N.M — concise summary" \
  --body-file workbench/part-N-item-M.md
```

The checked Markdown content-gate line is the non-interactive equivalent of
ticking the form checkbox: include it only because the gate was actually run in
Step 5. Do not use an unstructured `--body` as a shortcut: the API does not
render or enforce issue forms, so the draft itself must visibly carry every
required field and the checked content-gate acknowledgement. No disclosure or
fallback is needed merely because it was filed non-interactively; this is the
normal path.

For a **multi-item round**, file the round's parent issue first using the
feedback-round structure, read its number from the URL `gh issue create`
prints, set each item request's `round` to the real parent number and regenerate the validated body, file the items,
then link them by editing your own parent issue:

```bash
PARENT_URL="$(gh issue create --repo "$REPOSITORY" --title "Part N — feedback round from <solet-name>" \
  --body-file workbench/part-N-round.md)"
PARENT_NUMBER="${PARENT_URL##*/}"
# ... set round to the actual PARENT_NUMBER in each JSON request, regenerate,
# validate and show the exact resulting title/body for approval, then file:
CHILD_URL="$(gh issue create --repo "$REPOSITORY" --title "§N.M — concise summary" \
  --body-file workbench/part-N-item-M.md)"
# ... add "- [ ] #${CHILD_URL##*/} — §N.M — class — summary" to the parent draft's
# "Items in this round" list for every item, then regenerate and revalidate the parent and obtain approval before updating it:
gh issue edit "$PARENT_NUMBER" --repo "$REPOSITORY" --body-file workbench/part-N-round.md
```

You opened the parent, so GitHub lets you edit it. If the edit is refused, post
the same checklist as a comment on the parent with `gh issue comment`; any
account may comment. A single-item round needs no parent — file the one issue.

For a **design proposal**, use the feature-request form and put the design in
its body — the outcome needed, the alternatives considered, the tradeoffs, the
recommendation. Draft it under `workbench/` first if that helps you think, then
paste it in. Do not push a branch and do not open a pull request: the
maintainers cannot merge one. A design offer is accepted as information, not as
a roadmap commitment.

**If `gh` is missing or not signed in**, do not start a browser login flow and
do not install `gh` mid-round. Save each validated title/body separately under
`workbench/` (create the directory if absent), and optionally a round index
listing those files. Each body keeps its own leading envelope, then `Class:`
and optional `Round: #` anchors. Tell the operator the paths and chooser URL,
`https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/issues/new/choose` (owner and repository from
`git remote get-url origin`), and explain the forms/intake limitation above.
Current forms cannot preserve the leading envelope. Nothing is filed;
publication awaits a supported path that preserves the approved bytes.

<!-- not-attempt:start -->
**What you must NOT attempt.** Each of these fails for an account without write
access, and several fail silently:

- Do not pass `--label` to `gh issue create`, and do not follow it with
  `gh issue edit --add-label`. The first drops the label and reports success;
  the second is refused.
- Do not attach items through the sub-issues API (`gh api ... /sub_issues`). It
  answers `404` for an account without write access.
- Do not use `gh issue create --web`. It needs a browser, which a headless
  session does not have.
- Do not open a pull request, push a branch or send a patch. The repository
  accepts none of them.
- Do not ask for collaborator access, and do not re-file an issue to fix its
  labels.
<!-- not-attempt:end -->

## Step 8 — tell the operator what to expect

- An item is answered when its issue closes; the closing comment or linked
  commit says whether it was fixed, declined with a reason, already fixed since
  their release, or answered.
- Releases are the primary response surface. Subscribing to the repository's
  releases — not polling it — is how this deployment learns a round was
  answered, and how it learns a new release exists at all.
- Silence is not a disposition. Open items with no response by the next adopted
  re-mint get re-raised by number in the next round, briefly, as a list, not
  re-argued, referencing the still-open issues rather than filing duplicates.

## Rules

- **Never file without the operator's explicit yes.** Publication is
  irreversible in the way that matters: the content is out, and editing does
  not retract it.
- **Never hardcode the repository.** Resolve it from `origin` every time. The
  seed's home can move, and a hardcoded target sends the report nowhere.
- **One issue per item.** Each item needs its own disposition, and a merged
  issue can only be closed once.
- **Never fabricate an item number, an issue number, or a release identifier.**
  If a number is not known, look it up or leave it out and say so.
